# Git author map — Owner's order, 2026-08-05: "перепиши на GreenBear, закройте
# уже её раз и навсегда".
#
# THE PROBLEM. 507 commits on main are authored `YourName <youremail@example.com>`
# — git's out-of-the-box placeholder, left on a machine where nobody had run
# `git config user.name`. 124 in June 2026, 352 in July, 31 in August. The
# placeholder is not a person and never carried authorship information: it says
# only that the tool was unconfigured that day.
#
# WHAT THIS FILE DOES. Maps that placeholder to GreenBear, so `git log`,
# `git shortlog` and `git blame` show the Owner's account instead of a stranger.
# This is the attribution the Owner instructed, and it is recorded here as HIS
# decision rather than presented as a measurement — no measurement could recover
# what the placeholder threw away.
#
# WHY NOT REWRITE THE COMMITS THEMSELVES. Rewriting authorship for real means
# rewriting all 1681 commits from the oldest placeholder forward, with a new
# hash for every one. 358 commit hashes in config/release_journal.py alone would
# stop resolving, along with the board evidence, the audits and AGENTS.md, and
# every other checkout of this repository would need a hard reset. Mailmap is
# git's own answer to this exact question and costs none of that. Section 6
# forbids history rewriting without the Owner's word; this route does not need
# it and reaches the same visible result.
#
# Verified when added: `git log --use-mailmap` over main returns zero commits
# authored YourName, against 507 before.
#
# Format: canonical name <canonical email> <email to replace>
GreenBear <bearcaveltd@gmail.com> <youremail@example.com>
